Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i've always wanted to have kids  i can clearly picture myself throwing a  ball with them in the park  or sitting at the kitchen table helping  them with their homework  just like my dad used to with me  and i'm not just a future dad who really  wants to spend quality time with my  children  i'm also ambitious and dream of having a  high powered career  how about you how many of you are also  planning to have children  and would love to continue to pursue  your career  i see a lot of nodding heads and that  makes sense  more than three-quarters of us are  likely to become parents at some point  but despite so many of us wanting to be  the best parents that we can be  our current workplaces are not really  set up to allow us to be both great  parents  and pursue the careers that we want  even before covert most parents were  unhappy with their working situation  75 percent of working fathers  said that they would prefer to spend  more time at home with their children if  they had the chance  and 65 of stay at home mothers  actually said that they would like to  continue in paid work if they had the  opportunity  and even for those of you that aren't  planning to have kids these issues  should still matter to you  many of your friends family  and colleagues will parenting  is something that affects all of us  children are also negatively affected by  our current work  structures my work in early childhood  education  showed me first hand how important the  role of a parent is  in the first five years of a child's  development  research concludes that it's not whether  a parent works or not that matters most  to children  it's where the children have time at  home with their parents when they're  unencumbered by work stresses  children who had parents that were  overly invested in their career  tended to have more behavioral  difficulties than children whose parents  weren't  isn't this crazy so we know  that most of us are going to be parents  we know that we want to balance work and  family life  and we know that this affects the next  generation  and yet our inflexible work structures  are not set up to support us to pursue  both parenting and the careers we want  and for those of you who are thinking  well that's just the way it is we can't  change it  think back to a year ago i don't know  about you  but i had barely even heard of zoom  and now i spend more than half my life  on it  not only have we survived the world  going completely remote overnight  many organizations have actually thrived  something that we thought would have  been impossible a year ago  so let's leverage this moment let's  challenge  our mindsets and assumptions about how  we are meant to work  let's create a working society that  better supports parents  but what would a working society that  better supports parents actually look  like  well when i started my career in  consulting in australia  i was fortunate enough to experience  first hand  how a more flexible future could look  i worked on a project where i had two  direct managers  nicole and kirsten  they were doing what they described as a  job share  nicole worked monday to wednesday  kirsten  wednesday to friday with wednesday being  an overlap day  where they would both do handover to  each other  i must admit when i first started  working on this i was extremely  skeptical  i mean consulting is a pretty intense  job how was this going to work  but i couldn't have been more wrong the  next few months  were some of the highlights of my career  so far  both managers complimented each other  amazingly  one was a big picture thinker one  an analytical powerhouse basically  everyone was winning i  was getting two brains for the price of  one  the client was getting six days work for  the price of five  and nicole and kirsten were getting a  life that worked for them  they had found a way to continue working  in a  high-powered career that they loved  while also  being able to spend quality time at home  with their children  not only that but this work structure  meant that when they wanted to come back  to work full-time  the transition was so much easier  yet sadly experiences like this are rare  even when i tell people this story they  say things like wow  they're probably just good managers this  wouldn't work with everyone  this is exactly the mindset that i would  like to challenge  even if it's true that this isn't  possible right now  who's to say that it won't be possible  in the future  we are so innovative in so many areas of  our lives  and yet when it comes to the way that we  work  we seem to just blindly accept the  status quo  so what can we actually do to create a  working world that better supports  parents  today i'm going to talk about two  different strategies  first let's focus on the possibilities  rather than on the problems let's enable  more people  to do job shares like my managers did  let's allow more flexibility in the  times  and days that we work allowing people to  create schedules that work for them  and let's celebrate those who decide to  take a career break  or take parental leave to spend time  with their families  in effect let's iterate on our working  structures  in the same way that we iterate on new  product prototypes  let's leverage many of the modern tools  and technologies at our disposal  to overcome any problems that we come up  with  just as zoom and other tools have  enabled us to succeed over the past  year we're not going to get it right  first time  it might require us all to make some  changes  but in the end we will all be better off  for it  the second strategy let's change the way  that we show up to work  as leaders as managers and as  individuals  as leaders let's create policies that  better support parents  some examples  provide adequate parental leave  regardless of gender  and applaud those who decide to take you  up on it  we know how important the relationship  between parents and children is during  those first few years  so let's enable all parents to build  those relationships  consider providing subsidized child care  no one should ever have to choose to not  work because they're earning less than  the cost of their child care  your employees will be extremely  grateful but they will also be  much more productive and more likely to  stay with your company  as managers the next time you're  entrusted to manage someone  take the time to ask them about their  family situation  brainstorm ways that you could make the  workplace work for them  as well as for you and for the company  and finally as individuals push for this  yourself  as well just like my managers did  my fiance tess and i have had many  conversations about how we both want to  take time off work to spend with our  children when they're young  and how we both want to pursue  successful careers  we don't have all the answers yet but  these discussions  have helped us to define the type of  life that we want  and helped us to make decisions to help  us get there  with open dialogue drive and a dash of  ingenuity  let's fight the status quo be proactive  define what success looks like to you  talk candidly with those in your life  about it and encourage them to do the  same with you  as nicole and kirsten taught me all  those years ago  you never get what you don't ask for  so together let's build a better world  that where it is much easier to lead the  careers that we want to lead  as well as being the parents that we all  want to be thank you

